# Source: Exercise list "ICT3101 Exercises - Deductive Verification", problem 5 d), and Lecture Quiz 2.
id: minus-two
title: A loop that subtracts two
topic: Loops
level: 2
source: Exercise list "HoareLogic Exercises", problem 5 d; Lecture Quiz 2
tags: while, invariant, failed candidate, variant

task:
  Give a proof outline and a loop invariant. Give a variant for the loop.

pre: x = n && n > 0
post: x <= 0

program:
  {P}
  {I}
  while (x > 0) do {
    {I && x > 0}
    {R1}
    x := x - 2
    {I}
  }
  {I && !(x > 0)}
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: A first candidate that fails
rule: ASSIGN, LOGIC
text:
  The values of $x$ stay positive inside the loop, so a first idea is
  $x >= 0$. We check preservation.
calc:
  wp(x := x - 2, x >= 0)
  = x - 2 >= 0
  = x >= 2
vc: x >= 0 && x > 0 ==> x >= 2
status: fails
counterexample: x = 1
why:
  From $x = 1$ the body gives $x = -1$, which is outside the candidate.
  An odd initial value makes the loop stop at $-1$, so the candidate is too
  strong.

step: The corrected invariant
rule: WHILE-CONSEQUENCE
focus: while
text:
  If $x$ starts even, the loop stops at $0$. If $x$ starts odd, the last
  iteration goes from $1$ to $-1$. So every value of $x$ at a loop head is
  at least $-1$.
set: I = x >= -1

step: Establishment
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
vc: x = n && n > 0 ==> x >= -1

step: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN, CONSEQUENCE
focus: x := x - 2
calc:
  wp(x := x - 2, I)
  = x - 2 >= -1
  = x >= 1
set: R1 = x >= 1
vc: x >= -1 && x > 0 ==> x >= 1
why:
  For integers, $x > 0$ is $x >= 1$.

step: The postcondition at the exit
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
vc: x >= -1 && !(x > 0) ==> x <= 0
why:
  $!(x > 0)$ is $x <= 0$ itself.

step: Termination
rule: WHILE-TOTAL
text:
  The variable $x$ decreases by two, and it is positive whenever the body
  runs. So $x$ is a variant.
vc: x >= -1 && x > 0 ==> x >= 0
vc: {x >= -1 && x > 0 && x = E0} x := x - 2 {x < E0}
result: The triple is valid, and the loop terminates.

summary:
  The proof shows a weaker property than what the loop really does. The
  precise behaviour is: the loop ends with $x = 0$ when $n$ is even and
  with $x = -1$ when $n$ is odd. To prove it, the invariant also has to
  record that $x$ keeps the parity of $n$, for example with
  $x >= -1 && even(x - n)$, and the postcondition becomes
  $(even(n) ==> x = 0) && (odd(n) ==> x = -1)$.
